An expectation of boons and blessings. Things believed to be true before there is
evidence, written down so that when the evidence arrives it is clear which
beliefs it confirmed and which it demolished.

--

I believe a map with no words on it will turn out to be **more** legible than one
with labels, not less. The painting is already carrying an enormous amount of
information in its roofs and water and walls, and every word laid over it would
be competing with that rather than adding to it. The bet is that pressing space
and typing a name is a smaller cost than a permanently noisier city.

I believe hand-defining several hundred blocks over this painting is worth doing,
and that it will feel worth doing while it is being done rather than only
afterward. The whole project rests on that, and it is the belief most likely to
be demolished — probably somewhere around the two hundredth block, in the packed
quarter south-west of the bridge where one roof does not visibly end where the
next begins.

I believe the per-block default filter will be the best idea in the design. A
place that hands you its own lens when you arrive is doing something no other
map does, and overruling that lens is a small act of insight the game can notice.
Nothing else in the interface teaches you how to look.

I believe "the time is only ever now" will hold, and that every future pressure
to make the clock run by itself should be resisted. It is the sentence that turned
the map from a diagram into a model of somebody's understanding, and a live clock
would quietly take that back.

I believe the weave will look better than it sounds. Basket-weave hatching over a
painted city is the kind of thing that reads as either beautiful or as visual
noise with nothing in between, and I expect beautiful, on the grounds that it is
how cartographers solved this exact problem with ink.

I believe adjacency will turn out to be a better model of influence than any
radius would have been, and that the walls in this city will do real work in the
game because of it. A rumour that cannot cross a rampart is a more interesting
rumour.

I believe the interface being designed before the mechanics will constrain the
mechanics usefully rather than painfully. A game whose map cannot carry text and
cannot express a distance has to invent different systems than one that can, and
different is the point.
